      "Proteinuria is quantified by a **first-morning urine albumin-creatinine ratio (A1 under 30, A2 30-300, A3 over 300 mg/g)** or protein-creatinine ratio, not by dipstick; **nephrotic syndrome** is **proteinuria over 3.5 g/day (PCR over 3000 mg/g) with albumin under 3.0 g/dL, oedema and hyperlipidaemia** from a non-inflammatory podocyte lesion with bland sediment, while **nephritic syndrome** is **haematuria with red cell casts, hypertension, oliguria and rising creatinine** from glomerular inflammation - the child aged 1-12 with typical nephrotic syndrome gets **prednisolone 60 mg/m2/day without biopsy**, while every adult nephrotic, every non-resolving nephritic and any rapidly progressive GN is referred for biopsy.",
